  "theory": {
        "intro": "Since $180^{\\circ} = \\pi\\text{ radians}$, multiply degrees by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{180^{\\circ}}$ to get radians, and multiply radians by $\\dfrac{180^{\\circ}}{\\pi}$ to get degrees. Exact radian measures are written as simplified fractions of $\\pi$.",
        "facts": [
          "$180^{\\circ} = \\pi\\text{ radians}$, giving $1^{\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{180}\\text{ rad}$ and $1\\text{ rad} = \\dfrac{180^{\\circ}}{\\pi} \\approx 57.3^{\\circ}$.",
          "**Degrees to radians**: multiply by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{180^{\\circ}}$ and simplify the fraction: $\\text{rad} = \\text{deg} \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{180}$.",
          "**Radians to degrees**: multiply by $\\dfrac{180^{\\circ}}{\\pi}$: $\\text{deg} = \\text{rad} \\times \\dfrac{180}{\\pi}$.",
          "When converting a $\\pi$-fraction from radians to degrees, substitute $180^{\\circ}$ directly in place of $\\pi$.",
          "If an angle does not have a degree symbol ($^{\\circ}$), it is assumed to be in radians.",
          "Exact angles must be left as irreducible $\\pi$-fractions (e.g. $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$), not decimal approximations."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Identify the starting unit (degrees or radians)",
          "Multiply by the conversion factor $\\frac{\\pi}{180^{\\circ}}$ or $\\frac{180^{\\circ}}{\\pi}$",
          "Simplify the fraction or round as requested"
        ]
      },
